    "description": "The Account Server keeps user sessions alive inside a long-running process, while the Session Handler receives an object representing the signed-in user's preferences, active device list, and messaging service. On logout, the Account Server must invalidate the handler's ability to use that session immediately, even if the handler continues running or has copied the object reference elsewhere. Active administrative sessions and the underlying user account must not be destroyed, and a later login must receive a fresh session rather than a resurrected one. Logout is issued by a separate session-control service and must not depend on the handler acknowledging a message. Calls already in progress may complete, but any call started after logout must be rejected, including calls through related objects returned from the original session.",
    "decision_statement": "Select the approach that lets an independent control service invalidate future session use after logout without destroying the account or requiring the handler's cooperation.",
    "notes": "Independent logout invalidation without handler cooperation or account destruction grounds single-reference revocation; membrane is the strong near miss via the related-objects conformance clause."
  },
